 been determined. Up until now, the weight spectra of the Reed--Muller codes $RM(r,n)$ are still unknown for $r=3,...,n-7$. In this paper, we determine the exact weight spectrum of $\\RM(m-7, m)$ for every $m\\ge 14$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubsystems, Perron numbers, and continuous homomorphisms of Bernoulli shifts | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231942709_Subsystems_Perron_numbers_and_continuous_homomorphisms_of_Bernoulli_shifts)\nciteturn5search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; These numbers are precisely the spectral radii of nonnegative aperiodic integral matrices, and they possess an interesting arithmetic much like the natural numbers. Motivated by applications to symbolic dynamics and coding theory, we prove that the spectral radius of an aperiodic matrix whose nonzero entries are Perron is also a Perron number.\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nMatrices of Perron numbers\n\nArticle\n\n  * Feb 1992\n\n  * Douglas Lind\n\nA Perron number is an algebraic integer ≥1 that is strictly greater than the absolute value of its other conjugates. These numbers are precisely the spectral radii of nonnegative aperiodic integral matrices, and they possess an interesting arithmetic much like the natural numbers. Motivated by applications to symbolic dynamics and coding theory, we prove that the spectral radius of an aperiodic matrix whose nonzero entries are Perron is also a Perron number. Thus the set of Perron numbers is the “closure” of the natural numbers under the operation of taking spectral radii of aperiodic matrices with nonzero entries in the set. For a given nonnegative aperiodic integral matrix, we also obtain, by use of Diophantine arguments, an explicit upper bound on the smallest eigenvector in the dominant direction in which every entry is a Perron number.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nPositive matrices and dimension groups affiliated to C *-algebras and topological Markov chains\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jan 1981\n\n  * David Handelman\n\nView\n\nContinuous Homomorphisms of Bernoulli Schemes\n\nChapter\n\n  * Jan 1981\n  * ERGOD THEOR DYN SYST\n\n  * Andrés del Junco\n  * M. Keane\n  * Bruce Kitchens\n  * Laif Swanson\n\nLet m and n be integers greater than one. We set \\begin{array}{*{20}c} {{\\text{A = \\{ 0,1,}} \\ldots {\\text{,m - 1\\} }}}{{\\text{B}} = \\{ 0,1 \\ldots ,{\\text{n - l}}\\} } \\\\ {{\\text{X = A}}^\\mathbb{Z} }{{\\text{Y = B}}^\\mathbb{Z} } \\\\ {{\\text{S = left shift on X}}}{{\\text{T = left shift on Y}}{\\text{.}}} \\\\ \\end{array} then S and T are homeomorphisms of the compact spaces S and Y.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nNon-negative matrices and Markov chains. Revised reprint of the 2nd ed\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVolume 25, Issue 4 (2018) | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/issue/view/Volume25-4)\nciteturn5search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * The Matching Polynomials and Spectral Radii of Uniform Supertrees\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications, Volume 21 (https://dblp.org/db/journals/siammax/siammax21.html)\nciteturn5search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Spectral Radii of Fixed Frobenius Norm Perturbations of Nonnegative Matrices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMahler Problem: Arithmetic Rigidity and Beyond (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/mahler-problem)\nciteturn5search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Purity theorems then separate algebraic relations coming from independent points and from multiplicatively independent spectral radii, yielding strong algebraic independence statements for values of Mahler functions and, in particular, new proofs and generalizations o